---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const ACQ = "server/src/iki_server/files/acquired.py";

const H = {
  short: "Core stores every format; plugins understand them",
  how: "Where format knowledge lives",
  ref: "Each format, and who handles it",
  rules: "Limits and rules",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      This page answers: which file formats does Iki handle, and is it core or a plugin that does?
      Core stores any file, of any format, checked against its SHA-256 (<PageLink
        to="Where data is stored"
      />). Core also reads a few formats itself: the acquisition date that instruments stamp in
      their files, spreadsheets for import, FASTA in the browser for the alignment viewer, and
      GenBank and FASTA when it writes sequence records out. Everything else a lab means by "parsing
      a file" is a plugin's worker action: a script that reads the file on a worker and writes typed
      records (<Adr n={[6, 52]} />).
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>There is no central format registry. A field or param names the media types it takes.</li>
      <li>Parsed content is stored as typed fields of records in Postgres, not as file sidecars.</li>
      <li>Iki's own viewers are configured, not coded: a type names fields for them (<Adr n="0085" />).</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Media types by extension.</strong> Browsers don't know most scientific formats, so
        the web app maps extensions to media types (<code>.pdb</code> to <code>chemical/x-pdb</code>,{" "}
        <code>.ab1</code> to <code>application/x-abi</code>) and that wins over the browser's guess
        (<Path p="web/app/src/media.ts" />). The worker guesses a result file's type the same way
        when a plugin doesn't state it (<Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/files.rs" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fields and params.</strong> A file field or file param says which{" "}
        <code>media_types</code> it takes and its <code>max_size</code>; the server refuses others (<Adr
          n={[36, 52]}
        />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Acquisition dates.</strong> Core reads when an instrument says the data was acquired,
        from the file's own header, by ranged reads of at most 4 MiB, never the upload time (<Path
          p={ACQ}
        />, <Adr n="0177" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Viewers.</strong> A type's <code>display.viewers</code> names fields for Iki's{" "}
        <code>sequence</code>, <code>structure</code> and <code>alignment</code> viewers (<Path
          p="spec/README.md"
        />). Plugins' own views run in sandboxed frames (<PageLink to="Plugin frames" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Parsers.</strong> Each plugin's <code>scripts/</code> hold the readers its actions
        run on workers (<PageLink to="Launching a run" />).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <p>
      "Core" means the server, web app or worker does it for every deployment; a plugin name means
      it happens only where that plugin is installed. Every format is also stored as a plain file.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Core</th><th>Plugins</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Any file</td>
            <td>Stored once per SHA-256, downloaded as an attachment under its name (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/files/storage.py" sym="def content_disposition(" />)</td>
            <td>Fields such as an OOS investigation's attachments take PDFs and images (<Path p="plugins/qc_release/types/oos_investigation.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CSV, XLSX</td>
            <td>The spreadsheet importer: map columns, preview, then commit in one run. Legacy and encrypted workbooks are refused (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/spreadsheets/workbooks.py" />, <Adr n="0178" />)</td>
            <td><code>iki/plate_assays</code> reads a BMG LABTECH MARS endpoint export and joins it to wells (<Path p="plugins/plate_assays/scripts/reader.py" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FASTA, GenBank, SnapGene <code>.dna</code></td>
            <td>Writes a sequence record as GenBank or FASTA (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/sequence_files.py" />, <Adr n="0171" />)</td>
            <td><code>iki/dna</code> imports them as DNA sequences (<Path p="plugins/dna/actions/import_sequences.yaml" />); <code>iki/protein</code> imports protein FASTA (<Path p="plugins/protein/actions/import_proteins.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FASTA alignments</td>
            <td>The alignment viewer parses them in the browser with iki-wasm, up to 64 MiB (<Path p="worker/crates/iki-wasm/src/lib.rs" sym="pub const MAX_BYTES: usize = 64 * 1024 * 1024;" />)</td>
            <td>Alignment actions in <code>iki/dna</code> and <code>iki/protein</code> write them</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AB1 (Sanger traces)</td>
            <td>Acquisition date from <code>RUND</code>/<code>RUNT</code> (<Path p={ACQ} sym="def _abif(" />)</td>
            <td><code>align_reads</code> trims and aligns them to a reference (<Path p="plugins/dna/scripts/reads.py" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FASTQ, gzipped or not</td>
            <td>Stored or registered in place</td>
            <td><code>iki/sequencing</code>'s read sets (up to 100 GiB a file) and QC summaries (<Path p="plugins/sequencing/types/read_set.yaml" />, <Path p="plugins/sequencing/scripts/fastq.py" />); <code>iki/plasmid_sequencing</code>'s whole-plasmid checks (<Adr n={[196, 208]} />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SAM, BAM</td>
            <td>Acquisition date from the header's <code>@RG DT</code> (<Path p={ACQ} sym="def _sam(" />)</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>VCF, featureCounts, AIRR TSV</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td><code>iki/sequencing_analysis</code> imports a result: the file kept, a bounded preview, the selected sample's rows (<Path p="plugins/sequencing_analysis/scripts/import_result.py" />, <Adr n="0197" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>mzML and raw MS files</td>
            <td>mzML's acquisition date (<Path p={ACQ} sym="def _mzml(" />)</td>
            <td><code>iki/proteomics</code>'s MS files, up to 500 GiB, registered in place (<Path p="plugins/proteomics/types/ms_file.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>mzTab, MaxQuant, DIA-NN, FragPipe</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td><code>iki/proteomics</code> imports search reports (<Path p="plugins/proteomics/actions/import_search.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>LC-MS peak lists, peptide maps (TSV)</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td><code>iki/biologics_ms</code> compares them with registered masses (<Path p="plugins/biologics_ms/actions/characterize.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>PDB, mmCIF</td>
            <td>The structure viewer (Mol*, in a sandboxed frame), up to 256 MiB a file (<Path p="web/app/src/pages/structure-viewer.tsx" sym="export const MAX_STRUCTURE_BYTES = 256 * 1024 * 1024;" />, <Adr n="0085" />)</td>
            <td><code>iki/protein</code> imports and fetches structures (<Path p="plugins/protein/actions/import_structure.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SDF, MOL</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td><code>iki/small_molecule</code> imports and exports compounds with RDKit, and keeps conformer sets (<Path p="plugins/small_molecule/actions/import_sdf.yaml" />, <Path p="plugins/small_molecule/actions/export_sdf.yaml" />, <Path p="plugins/small_molecule/types/conformer_set.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FCS, JCAMP-DX, TIFF and OME-TIFF, JPEG</td>
            <td>Acquisition date only (<Path p={ACQ} sym="def _fcs(" />, <Path p={ACQ} sym="def _jcamp(" />, <Path p={ACQ} sym="def _image(" />)</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reference catalogues (MGI reports and others)</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td>Registration actions such as <code>iki/mouse_strains</code>' (<Path p="plugins/mouse_strains/actions/register_mgi_strain.yaml" />)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Gels</td>
            <td>Drawn from computed fragments, not stored as images (<Path p="web/app/src/pages/entity-gel.tsx" />, <Adr n="0202" />)</td>
            <td>Digests in <code>iki/plasmid</code> compute the fragments</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="What a parser may assume" open>
        <p>
          A parser runs on a worker, never in the server or the page. It gets the run's files
          already checked against their size and SHA-256 (<PageLink to="How data links to records" />), and
          writes what it read as records, each of which may say which file and record it came from
          (<Adr n="0052" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Spreadsheets are untrusted">
        <p>
          Only cell values are read; nothing in a workbook runs, macros included. Before parsing,
          the importer checks the first bytes, counts decompressed bytes against a limit and a
          ratio (zip bombs), and refuses XML with a document type. A workbook is at most 100 MiB,
          with at most a million rows and 1,000 columns a sheet (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/spreadsheets/workbooks.py"
            sym="MAX_ROWS = 1_000_000"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Acquisition dates are never guessed">
        <p>
          A date is only as precise as the file states it, with an offset only when the file gives
          one. A malformed or ambiguous date (a two-digit year, <code>DD/MM/YY</code>) is unknown,
          not guessed. A file without one keeps the date its uploader gave, or stays unknown (<Path
            p={ACQ}
          />, <Adr n="0177" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Planned">
        <p>
          On the roadmap after M1, each to be scoped: workers on the lab network that reach
          instruments and file shares, with readings kept as an append-only series; a sequence
          workbench that shows Sanger chromatograms; a structure workbench on Mol*; and finding a
          phrase inside a file (<Path p="docs/roadmap.md" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
